xact checks. Let me read validate_decomposition.py and frontmatter.py schema.\n\nAlso gate_failure_impact for gated epics:\n- E003: gated_by=E001, action=rewrite, fallback: \"If workspace resolution proves coupled to tracking store initialization, the server-side initialization path and plugin refactoring scope are rewritten per the investigation findings; path-to-experiment resolution may require an alternative lookup mechanism.\"\n- E004: gated_by=E001, action=rewrite, fallback: \"If plugin workspace resolution is coupled to full tracking server initialization, the epic's scope expands from verification to refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path.\"\n- E005: gated_by=E002, action=rewrite, fallback: \"If routing requires tracking-server-side delegation configuration rather than gateway-level routing, the operator topology and routing configuration are redesigned accordingly.\"\n\nWait — E003 depends on both E001 (gated) and E004. dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E001,RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\". gated_by=E001.\n\nE005 dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E002,RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\", gated_by=E002.\n\nHmm, wait — should E005 depend on E003? Let me reconsider Rule 13 once more. \"Implementation producing artifact another epic's code builds against (API, CRD, library) → consuming Implementation serial. Does NOT apply to configuration references.\" The operator templates the server's container command with flags — is a CLI flag a \"configuration reference\" or an \"API the code builds against\"? The Helm template literally contains the flag strings — configuration. Like the image digest example: the operator epic could get an AC \"the rendered artifacts-only Deployment runs the server with the combined flags (supported by the server-side change)\" rather than an edge. Hmm. But the routing configuration — \"configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that instance\" — depends on whether the server needs a config change (E002 outcome). And the operator's HTTPRoute design routes artifact traffic — the server's artifact proxy behavior matters.\n\nArguments for parallel (no edge): operator chart development is configuration; the server flags already exist individually (`--artifacts-only` exists, `--enable-workspaces` exists) — the operator could template them before the server supports the combo, gated by AC on first execution. Arguments for serial: the operator's deliverable (correctly functioning split topology) requires the server capability; E002's outcome may require server changes that alter the operator's routing design.\n\nGiven E002 already gates E005 and E002's outcome incorporates whether the server needs changes, and the flag strings are configuration references... I think the safer decomposition per the rules: E005 gets an AC (\"the artifacts-only Deployment's rendered command uses the combined flag combination supported by the server-side change; end-to-end artifact serving through the split topology validates workspace enforcement\") instead of a hard E003 → E005 edge? Hmm.\n\nBut Rule 13's spirit: \"Code builds against an API, CRD, or library\". The operator code builds against the MLflow CR and Helm charts, not against the server's Python code. The server's CLI flags are configuration the operator renders — analogous to image digests/RELATED_IMAGE. Per the explicit guidance in Rule 13 (\"configuration references (image digests, endpoint URLs) — those are AC gates\"), the flag combination and the artifacts-only instance endpoint are configuration references. So NO E003 → E005 edge; instead AC on E005.\n\nHmm, but \"endpoint URLs\" — routing artifact traffic to the dedicated instance's Service is exactly an endpoint URL configuration. Yes — I'm now fairly convinced: E005 should NOT have a hard dependency on E003. AC gate instead: \"the split topology serves and workspace-validates artifacts end-to-end using the workspace-aware artifacts-only server capability (combined flags), verified in integration\". That keeps operator work parallel with server work, gated by E002 only.\n\nBut then end-to-end testing on E005 requires E003 done — that's fine as AC gate (\"verified once the server-side capability lands\"). DAG: E002 → E005 only. And E001 → E004 → E003.\n\nCritical path: E001 → E004 → E003 (length 3). Or E002 → E005 (length 2). Critical path length = 3.\n\nHmm wait, but does E003 depend on E004 really? Rule 12: framework/library → consumer. The server's artifact request handling calls the plugin's workspace resolution. The plugin already exists with an interface; E004 extends it for artifacts-only context. If E004 refactors (e.g., factors resolution into independent init path), the server's calling code may change. The server epic \"delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\" — it builds against the plugin's resolution interface. I'll keep E004 → E003 per Rule 12. Hmm, but one could also argue the server epic's delegation uses the existing interface unchanged... The strategy says \"The artifacts-only proxy needs to call the same workspace resolution logic\" — the coupling is real. Keep the edge.\n\nActually hold on — reconsider. Both E003 and E004 are gated by E001. If E001 finds the plugin needs refactoring, E004 does it, then E003 builds the server calling the refactored path. If not coupled, E004 is mostly verification. Either way E003's delegation code is written against the plugin's resolution surface. Edge justified.\n\nSo final DAG:\n- E001 (Investigation, P0) → E003, E004\n- E002 (Investigation, P1) → E005\n- E004 (Impl, P0) → E003\n- E003 (Impl, P0)\n- E005 (Impl, P1)\n\nepic_count=5, critical_path_length=3 (E001→E004→E003).\n\nPriorities: E001 P0 (blocks P0). E002 P1. E003 P0. E004 P0 (inherits: enables P0 HLR-2 via E003; also its own HLR-7 is P1 — inherits highest = P0). E005 P1.\n\nWait, E004 → E003 edge means E004 blocks all P0 server work → implicitly P0. Yes.\n\nPriority check for validator: \"an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0\" — E001 blocks P0 (E003/E004) → P0 ✓. E002 blocks E005 (P1) → P1 ✓. E004 blocks E003 (P0) → P0 ✓.\n\nNow, per-epic files, ai-signals files (one per epic: E001-E005 → 5 signal files), decomposition summary.\n\nEpic numbering: order them logically: E001 = workspace resolution investigation, E002 = routing investigation, E003 = server implementation, E004 = plugin implementation, E005 = operator implementation.\n\nHmm, one more consideration on E001's component: it spans MLflow server + plugin. Most relevant canonical: \"MLflow\". Team: MLflow Team.\n\nE002 component: \"MLflow\" (operator work). Team: MLflow Team.\n\nNow let me check the frontmatter schema and validator script before writing.","signature":"f2776b1bc39d4785a0034a584584e726"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39525,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4f627a192accfe8","agent_id":"aa1207370f11fae22","session_id":"d88b54d0-1f1c-4737-bb2c-840a712cae3c","uuid":"b1e80043-dae7-4425-ab6b-c0758aca9161","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:50:21.450Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
