{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b8827b9b-fd47-4b6c-8cde-7bf1cf0cc91d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-85237580c67fc285","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-wm0i13tu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E001.md","content":"# Investigate workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves whether MLflow workspace resolution — experiment-to-workspace mapping\nand authorization through the existing auth plugin infrastructure — can function in a server\nstarted with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces`, where tracking stores and model registry\nstores are not initialized. The strategy flags two genuine unknowns that gate the downstream\nserver and plugin implementation scope:\n\n1. **Plugin initialization coupling**: does workspace store initialization in\n   `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` depend on tracking store availability? The strategy's risk table\n   notes workspace resolution may be coupled to the tracking server's full initialization path; if\n   so, the plugin requires refactoring to factor workspace resolution into an independent\n   initialization path.\n2. **Artifact path resolution**: do artifact request paths carry sufficient information\n   (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership without tracking stores? Artifacts\n   are stored under experiment-scoped paths (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`) in the standard\n   S3 configuration, but the strategy flags this assumption as needing validation, including\n   limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\nBoth questions are answered by the same experiment: standing up workspace resolution in a\nstore-less server context and tracing the plugin's initialization dependencies, so they are\ncombined into one investigation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` workspace provider initialization path (registered through\n  the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`) to\n  determine whether it depends on tracking store or model registry store initialization.\n- Determine whether the `kubernetes://` workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping\n  accessible without initializing tracking stores.\n- Validate that artifact request paths in the artifacts API surface can be resolved to their parent\n  experiment (and thus workspace) in the standard S3 artifact store configuration, and document\n  limitations for non-standard artifact roots or custom artifact root configurations.\n- Produce a findings report with a concrete refactoring plan if workspace resolution is coupled to\n  the full tracking server initialization path (factoring resolution into an independent\n  initialization path within the plugin).\n- Confirm or revise the downstream implementation scope for the server-side workspace validation\n  and the plugin work, and record the outcome signal (scope confirmed vs. rewrite needed).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A findings report answers, with pass/fail determinations and supporting evidence: (a) whether\n  plugin workspace store initialization depends on tracking store availability, and (b) whether\n  artifact request paths resolve to parent experiments without tracking stores in the standard S3\n  configuration.\n- If coupling exists, the report includes a concrete refactoring plan for an independent workspace\n  resolution initialization path in the plugin, sufficient to scope the plugin implementation work.\n- Limitations of artifact path resolution for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n- The downstream implementation scope for workspace-aware artifacts-only serving is explicitly\n  confirmed or revised based on the findings.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nThis investigation de-risks (does not itself cover) the following strategy HLRs:\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): flag combination for workspace-validated artifact serving — the feasibility outcome\n  determines the server implementation approach.\n- HLR-2 (P0): workspace-membership validation of artifact requests — depends on path-to-experiment\n  resolution and plugin resolution working without tracking stores.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior — the denial path builds on the same resolution machinery.\n- HLR-7 (P1): auth plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context — the coupling\n  question directly determines whether the plugin work is verification or refactoring.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The strategy's mitigation for the coupling risk is to validate the plugin's initialization\n  dependencies early (sprint 1 of the effort estimate); this investigation is that validation.\n- Scope is initially limited to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) where\n  artifact paths follow the well-defined experiment-scoped structure.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":65825,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4f627a192accfe8","agent_id":"aa1207370f11fae22","session_id":"d88b54d0-1f1c-4737-bb2c-840a712cae3c","uuid":"15661640-2d95-4fb4-b17b-b6c61f4924c5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:53:12.156Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
