{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-24f63fbf-eafb-4585-b63e-f94e1b76ef9c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab7ce56cdbfa13d7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-wm0i13tu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E003.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers server-side workspace-aware artifact serving in the MLflow tracking server by\nmaking `--artifacts-only` composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag. When both flags\nare set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace\nprovider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay\nuninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server\nresolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment\nvia the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure.\nRequests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403. The design is fail-closed: if the\nworkspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\nNo new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing\nflags (Staff Engineer / SME Input).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Server initialization: allow `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` to be combined; when\n  both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if\n  missing) and initialize only the workspace provider. Tracking and model registry stores remain\n  uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Artifact request validation: resolve each artifact request path to its parent experiment\n  (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`,\n  scoped initially to the S3 artifact store — the standard RHOAI configuration), look up the\n  experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the\n  existing auth plugin infrastructure (per Staff Engineer Input, without prescribing Kubernetes\n  API mechanics such as SelfSubjectAccessReview — those are plugin implementation details).\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable,\n  artifact requests are denied (not served without validation), returning an error indicating\n  workspace validation is unavailable.\n- Backwards compatibility: single-tenant deployments and configurations without\n  `--enable-workspaces` retain current artifacts-only behavior; omitting either flag preserves\n  current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging (P2, bundled): workspace access denials in artifact serving are logged through\n  server logs with sufficient detail to audit (denying user, workspace, artifact path). The\n  telemetry channel follows the HLR's server-logs specification; a redirect to another channel\n  (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log) would be follow-up scope.\n- Workspace resolution latency is measured and reported per artifact request; short-TTL caching of\n  workspace membership decisions is implemented only if measured overhead exceeds the threshold\n  agreed with PM/Engineering (open question — no latency target is specified in the strategy).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user\n  requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served —\n  measured by HTTP 200 response with correct artifact content.\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance and a user who is a member of workspace A but\n  not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B,\n  then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 response with an error message indicating\n  insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests\n  an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200\n  response regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running with the workspace store (Kubernetes\n  API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied (fail-closed) —\n  measured by HTTP 503 response indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n- The server fails to start with a clear error when `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces`\n  are both set without an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- Non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled when the combined flags are set, and tracking/model\n  registry stores are not initialized in this mode.\n- Workspace access denials are logged through server logs with auditing detail (user, workspace,\n  artifact path).\n- Single-tenant and non-workspace regression: deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces`\n  exhibit unchanged artifacts-only behavior (regression suite passes).\n- Workspace resolution latency per artifact request is measured and reported; the caching decision\n  (implement now vs. defer) is recorded against the agreed overhead threshold.\n- The workspace validation path exercises the auth plugin's workspace resolution in the\n  artifacts-only context (delegation verified against the plugin capability, not reimplemented in\n  the server).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and\n  `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n- HLR-2 (P0): ar