{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3b7cfbef4e5263a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n10\t\n11\t## Triage\n12\t\n13\tNot below threshold: the strategy is M-L (4-7 sprints per its own effort estimate), spans three\n14\tcomponents (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) even though a single team owns all\n15\tof them, and contains genuine unknowns (plugin initialization coupling, artifact traffic routing\n16\tdesign) that change what downstream epics do. Not documentation-only: all affected components\n17\trequire code changes. Full decomposition follows.\n18\t\n19\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n20\t\n21\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated\n22\ttechnical approach. Overrides applied:\n23\t\n24\t1. **No new flag** — the input rejects a `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and\n25\t   `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field; the correct approach is combining the existing\n26\t   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags. The strategy body already reflects this; all\n27\t   server and operator epic scope follows the combined-flags approach. No epic introduces a new\n28\t   server flag.\n29\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic scope is a\n30\t   dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic\n31\t   routing from the main tracking deployment, not a CR field / Helm values propagation only.\n32\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — the strategy (per the input) states that workspace\n33\t   authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API\n34\t   mechanics. Epic scope stays at that level; no epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other\n35\t   plugin-internal mechanics. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup\n36\t   are unchanged.\n37\t\n38\t## Component Mapping\n39\t\n40\tThe canonical component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains a single MLflow entry:\n41\t`MLflow`. The strategy's affected components map as follows (logged per the component-name\n42\tconstraint; `submit.py` silently strips non-canonical names):\n43\t\n44\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n45\t|----|----|----|\n46\t| MLflow | MLflow | Verbatim match |\n47\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | No canonical \"MLflow Operator\" entry; closest canonical name. The operator epic's span is noted in its body (split-server topology in the MLflow operator). |\n48\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | No canonical entry; closest canonical name. The plugin epic's span is noted in its body (auth plugin workspace resolution). |\n49\t\n50\tAll epics are owned by the MLflow Team (single team per the Impacted Teams table).\n51\t\n52\t## Epic List\n53\t\n54\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n55\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n56\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server artifact serving topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n61\t\n62\t## Dependency DAG\n63\t\n64\t```mermaid\n65\tgraph TD\n66\t    E001[\"E001 Workspace resolution feasibility — Investigation\"]\n67\t    E002[\"E002 Artifact traffic routing design — Investigation\"]\n68\t    E003[\"E003 Workspace-aware artifacts-only server — Implementation\"]\n69\t    E004[\"E004 Plugin workspace resolution for artifacts-only — Implementation\"]\n70\t    E005[\"E005 Operator split-server topology — Implementation\"]\n71\t    E001 --> E003\n72\t    E001 --> E004\n73\t    E004 --> E003\n74\t    E002 --> E005\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\t## DAG Justification\n78\t\n79\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|------|------|-----------|\n81\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether workspace resolution can function without tracking stores determines the server-side validation approach (path-to-experiment resolution, initialization path). True gate — `rewrite` if workspace resolution proves coupled to tracking store initialization or paths cannot resolve to experiments. |\n82\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The open question \"does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" determines whether the plugin epic is verification-only or a refactor of workspace resolution into an independent initialization path. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n83\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request validation delegates authorization to the plugin's workspace resolution surface; the server code builds against the (possibly refactored) plugin interface. |\n84\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing design determines the operator's HTTPRoute design and whether the main tracking deployment needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. True gate — `rewrite` if routing requires a different mechanism than assumed. |\n85\t| E003 —/→ E005 (no edge) | Rule 13 carve-out (configuration reference) | The combined server flags and the artifacts-only instance's Service endpoint that the operator renders are configuration references, not build-against artifacts (API/CRD/library). The coupling is expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on the operator epic: end-to-end workspace enforcement through the split topology is verified against the workspace-aware artifacts-only server capability. A DAG edge would serialize operator chart development that can proceed in parallel. |\n86\t\n87\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n88\t\n89\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n90\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n91\t| HLR-1 | P0 | `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Server-side flag composition; no new flag per Staff Engineer Input. |\n92\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003, RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Server enforces validation (403 path); plugin provides workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context. |\n93\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Fail-closed: workspace store unreachable → requests denied | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Denial on unreachable workspace store (HTTP 503 per strategy AC 4). |\n94\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Backwards-compatibility ACs; regression coverage. |\n95\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment with own Service and HTTPRoute | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server topology, not a config toggle (Staff Engineer Input). |\n96\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Routing mechanism determined by the routing-design investigation. |\n97\t| HLR-7 | P1 | Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Verification and (if needed) refactor of the plugin's initialization path. |\n98\t| HLR-8 | P2 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: denial audit logging is emitted from the same artifact-request denial code path that the P0 validation HLRs implement — it shares the same request-handling surface and data (user, workspace, artifact path); a split would double-touch the same files and build the same denial path twice. Not separately schedulable. |\n99\t\n100\t## Health Warnings\n101\t\n102\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the operator split-server topology epic is ~2-3 sprints by the\n103\t  strategy's own effort estimate — above the ~2-week epic-size guideline. Kept as one logical\n104\t  change: the dedicated artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates, workspace\n105\t  configuration propagation, and traffic-routing configuration all land in the same Helm chart and\n106\t  reconciler surface; a split would fragment a single chart extension and duplicate the rendering\n107\t  machinery. **Re-split trigger**: re-split the operator epic if the routing-design outcome or the\n108\t  chart work exceeds the estimated 2-3 sprints — e.g. split the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n109\t  deployment (Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates) from the main-tracking traffic-routing\n110\t  configuration if the two outgrow a single sprint each or land in different sprints.\n111\t- **Performance NFR has no latency target** (open question, owner PM/Engineering): whether\n112\t  short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions is required at launch or deferred cannot be\n113\t  answered before the workspace resolution exists. Not modeled as a blocking Investigation because\n114\t  the answer does not change which epics exist — caching is incremental scope inside the same\n115\t  server epic, not a different epic structure. The server epic carries a measurement-and-report AC;\n116\t  